course: biology
lesson: 13
title: Mountains: life as you climb higher
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Climbing a high mountain is like travelling from summer to the North Pole in one day: woods at the bottom, then meadows, then rocks, then snow even in July.
explain: Every 1000 metres up the air gets about 6 to 7 degrees colder, and it gets thinner, so each breath brings less oxygen and you puff and pant on the path.
explain: Up there the summer is short, the wind is fierce and the sunshine is strong, so trees give up at the treeline and only tough, low, hairy or cushion-shaped plants carry on.
explain: Mountain animals are champions of cold and thin air: shaggy yaks with big hearts and lungs, ibex with grippy hooves on cliffs, marmots whistling a warning, a snow leopard wrapped in its own tail like a scarf.
fact: The treeline is where trees stop on a mountain, because above it the summer is too short and cool and the wind too strong for trees to grow wood; it is about 4000 metres near the equator but only about 600 metres in Scotland.
fact: Air is thinner higher up, so each breath has less oxygen; at the top of Mount Everest a breath has only about a third of the oxygen it has at the beach, so most climbers carry oxygen bottles.
fact: Air gets colder by about 6 to 7 degrees for every 1000 metres you climb, which is why Kilimanjaro, almost on the equator, has snow and ice near its top; it is not because the top is nearer the Sun, which is about 150 million km away, so a few km closer makes no difference.
fact: Alpine ibex climb the almost vertical stone walls of dams, like the Cingino dam in Italy, to lick salt, gripping with hooves that have hard edges and soft pads; baby ibex can climb within days of being born.
fact: Marmots are big ground squirrels of mountain meadows that whistle when they see an eagle or a fox, so everyone dives into the burrows; Alpine marmots hibernate about six months a year.
fact: Yaks live on the Tibetan plateau, often above 4000 metres, with long thick hair, a woolly undercoat and big lungs and hearts, and they find weather above about 15 degrees hard.
fact: Snow leopards of Central Asia use their very long fluffy tail for balance on cliffs and as a warm wrap; they have small round ears and wide furry paws like snowshoes, and they cannot roar, only make a puffing sound.
fact: The Andean condor has wings about 3 metres across and glides on rising air; one was found to fly over 170 kilometres without flapping once.
fact: Edelweiss is covered in thick woolly white hairs that protect it from strong sunlight, cold and drying wind, and it is protected, so it must not be picked in many places.
fact: Llamas mostly spit at other llamas, not at people, to settle arguments over food or space; llamas and alpacas come from the Andes and are cousins of camels.
fact: Cushion plants grow in low tight domes that stay calmer and warmer inside, sometimes several degrees warmer than the air; some llareta cushions in the Andes may be over 1000 years old.
fact: Pikas are small round relatives of rabbits that stack grass into hay piles under rocks for winter, because they do not hibernate; they overheat easily, so warming mountains are a danger to them.
exercise: Why do trees stop growing at the treeline? | The summer is too short and cold and the wind too strong | The soil up there is poisonous to trees and kills their roots in the first year | Goats eat every tree above it | Trees are scared of heights
exercise: Where is the treeline higher? | Near the equator, about 4000 metres | In Scotland, about 4000 metres | At the North Pole | It is the same height everywhere
exercise: Why do you puff and pant on a high mountain path? | The air is thinner, so each breath has less oxygen | The air up there is full of thick dust from the rocks | Mountains pull harder on your legs | Cold air is too heavy for your lungs to breathe in properly
exercise: About how much oxygen does a breath at the top of Everest have, compared with the beach? | About a third | About the same | About twice as much | No oxygen at all
exercise: How much colder does the air get for every 1000 metres up? | About 6 to 7 degrees | About 1 degree | About 30 degrees | It gets warmer, closer to the Sun
exercise: Why does Kilimanjaro have snow although it is almost on the equator? | Its top is so high that the air there is very cold | Snow is carried there by the wind all the way from the North Pole | The Sun never shines on its top | It is made of ice inside
exercise: Why do ibex climb dam walls? | To lick salt and minerals off the stones | To escape from the big fish in the lake | To build their nests up there | To get a better view of the sunset over the mountains
exercise: What does a marmot's loud whistle mean? | Danger, an eagle or fox is near | Come and eat, food has been found | It is going to rain | Good morning to the sun
exercise: Why can a yak live where lowland cattle would struggle? | Long thick hair, a woolly undercoat and big lungs and hearts | It drinks warm milk all winter long | It sleeps all through the cold months | It carries extra oxygen in its hollow horns, like a diver's tank
exercise: What does a snow leopard use its long fluffy tail for? | Balance on cliffs and as a warm wrap | Swatting the flies away in summer | Catching fish in cold mountain streams | Hanging from trees by its tail like a monkey
exercise: How does the Andean condor fly so far without flapping? | It glides on rising warm air and wind up the slopes | It has a tiny engine of muscles in its tail that pushes it along | It flaps very fast where we cannot see | It only flies downhill
exercise: How wide are an Andean condor's wings? | About 3 metres | About 30 centimetres | About 10 metres | Only about 1 metre
exercise: Why is edelweiss covered in woolly white hairs? | To protect it from strong sun, cold and drying wind | To keep insects away from its seeds | To catch snowflakes to drink when they melt in the sun | To look pretty for walkers
exercise: Who does a llama usually spit at? | Other llamas, to settle arguments | Any person who comes up to stroke it | Its own babies, to clean their fur | The flies that keep landing on its nose
exercise: Why do mountain plants grow in low tight cushions? | It is calmer and warmer close to the ground | They are trying to hide from the hungry goats | Their seeds are too heavy to grow tall | Rocks stop their stems from growing upwards
exercise: Why does a pika stack hay under the rocks? | It does not hibernate, so it needs food under the snow | To build a soft bed for its babies in the spring | To hide from eagles inside the hay | To keep the rocks from rolling down the steep slope in a storm
exercise: Which animals are cousins of llamas? | Camels | Mountain sheep | Wild horses | Mountain goats
test: In a cartoon, a llama spits at every tourist who walks past. What is closer to the truth? | Llamas mostly spit at other llamas over food or space | Llamas spit at all people on sight | Llamas never spit at all | Llamas spit only at their keepers, who feed them every day
test: we're goin up a 3000 m mountain, it's 20 degrees down here. how cold at the top roughly | About 0 degrees, losing about 6 to 7 for every 1000 m | About 20 degrees, the same as down here | About 40 degrees, as it's nearer the Sun | About minus 60 degrees
test: Tom says it's colder on mountains because they are further from the Sun. Is he right? | No, the thin rising air cools, and a few kilometres are nothing next to the Sun's distance | Yes, every metre nearer the Sun is warmer | Yes, the Sun cannot reach mountain tops | No, mountains are actually warmer
test: What would happen to a pika that sat in hot sunshine all afternoon? | It could overheat and die | It would grow a thicker coat | It would fall asleep for the winter | Nothing, pikas love the heat
test: 5 marmots pop up on a rock. How many ears is that? | 10 | 5 | 20 | 15
test: A yak and a snow leopard: how many legs and hearts together? | 8 legs and 2 hearts | 8 legs and 4 hearts | 4 legs and 2 hearts | 6 legs and 2 hearts
test: Mia picks an edelweiss on an Alpine walk to take home. What is wrong? | It is protected and must not be picked in many places | Nothing, there are millions of them | It is poisonous to touch | It will turn into a weed at home and take over the whole garden
test: In a game, a snow leopard roars to scare off the player. What would a real one do? | It cannot roar; it makes a puffing sound | Exactly the same, they roar very loudly | It barks like a dog | It sings like a bird
test: Why might a climber on Everest carry oxygen bottles? | Each breath there has only about a third of the oxygen at sea level | Oxygen bottles keep them warm | There is no air at all above 5000 metres, so they could not breathe otherwise | The bottles help them float up the slope
test: Why do baby ibex not fall off the cliffs? | Their hooves have hard edges and grippy pads, and they climb within days of birth | Their mothers hold them by the ear | They stay in caves until they are grown | They have claws like a cat
test: If summers in the mountains get warmer year after year, what may happen to the treeline? | It may creep higher up the mountain | It will drop down to the valley | It will stay exactly where it is now | All the trees will vanish
exam: Identify the principal reason for which mountaineers experience breathlessness at high altitude. | The reduced density of the air diminishes the oxygen obtained in each breath | The proximity of the Sun renders the air excessively warm for respiration | The force of gravity is substantially greater upon mountains | The air at altitude contains a greater proportion of dust
exam: Ascertain the approximate decrease in air temperature that accompanies an ascent of one thousand metres. | Approximately six to seven degrees Celsius | Approximately one degree Celsius | Approximately thirty degrees Celsius | The temperature increases rather than decreases
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the treeline? | The altitude above which the season is too brief and cold for trees to grow | A row of trees deliberately planted by foresters along each slope of a mountain | The boundary beyond which mountain goats consume all trees | The point at which the soil becomes poisonous to vegetation
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the vocalisation of the snow leopard. | It is incapable of roaring and produces a puffing sound | It roars more loudly than any other cat | It communicates exclusively by whistling | It produces no sound of any description
